## Work State
### Completed
- Read and audited `src/runtime.rs` parser/formatting region, `src/stdlib.rs` registry/signatures, `SPEC.md` time section, `README.md`, `SURVEY.md`, `phases/phase-097/REPORT.md`, `tests/time_builtin_test.rs`, `tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs`, `tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt`, `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs`.
- Found no reachable panic/UB path in the new parser: all indexing uses ASCII-controlled bounds or `get`; year parse errors are handled; offsets/fractions are bounded; `from_ymd_opt`/`from_hms_nano_opt` return `Option`.
- Confirmed registry counts, signatures, dotted-call dispatch, and doc-block anchors.
- Candidate regression identified: old `time.unix` used `chrono::NaiveDateTime::parse_from_str(text, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")`, which likely accepted single-digit fields (`%m`, `%d`, `%H`, `%M`, `%S`) and flexible whitespace; new `parse_iso8601` requires exactly two digits and exactly one `T`/space. This contradicts REPORT claims “existing … text behaviour is unchanged” / “old callers keep their answers.” No test pins the old acceptance.
- Candidate inconsistency: numeric `time.format` now renders `%Z`/`%z`/`%+` as UTC offset instead of empty, while REPORT says the numeric form returns exactly what it did before. The new test explicitly expects UTC.
- Candidate SPEC drift: `SPEC.md` says `time.parse` accepts “these ISO-8601 shapes, and nothing else” while also documenting 1-3 digit years, which are not ISO-8601; `time.unix` also applies offsets through the shared parser though the spec sentence mentions offset application only for `time.parse`.

### Active
- Drafting final review findings and deciding severities.
- Most likely reportable finding: `MAJOR` at `src/runtime.rs:717` for narrowing `time.unix` text acceptance; cannot mark `BLOCKER` because old chrono behaviour could not be executed/verified.
- Possible secondary findings: `MINOR` at `src/runtime.rs:929`/`1668` for numeric `%Z` behaviour/REPORT contradiction; `MINOR` at `SPEC.md:1517`/`1532` for “ISO-8601 and nothing else” vs 1-3 digit years.

